 \begin{abstract}
-A face morph detector can separate benchmark morphs from genuine photographs without detecting the combination of two identities. We investigate this ambiguity through morph-free training, matched manipulation controls, and training ablations. Networks trained only to distinguish synthetic faces from shrink-and-enlarge copies achieve zero equal error rate on the native MorDIFF evaluation in all ten tested networks. We introduce comparisons that progressively control rendering, warping, blending, and contributor identity, revealing substantial responses to single-photo reconstructions and no established advantage beyond low-level statistics in a matched blend-versus-warp diagnostic. We then study training with face-spliced morphs and matched same-person and seam-only negatives. A count-balanced development ablation improves two-person versus same-person ranking by 0.048 AUC across five seeds; negative content remains confounded with 312 additional unique images. The full protocol attenuates storage-induced false alarms. On development-exposed MAD22, it achieves 3.37\% mean EER versus SelfMAD's 4.72\%, without establishing superiority. Together, the results provide an evaluation framework and a qualified training benefit while identifying the evidence still needed for identity-specific detection.
